# Safety Veins - AI Module

Four files, three layers, working together:

## 1. `generate_dataset.py` — synthetic training data
Generates realistic mine sensor time-series (gas buildup curves, O2 drops,
heat spikes, fall events) — NOT random noise. Run this first:
```
python generate_dataset.py
```
Produces `mine_sensor_dataset.csv`.

## 2. `fusion_classifier.py` — sensor fusion risk model (scikit-learn)
Combines ALL sensors into one risk probability instead of judging each
sensor alone. Works fully offline on a Raspberry Pi — no GPU needed.
```
pip install pandas numpy scikit-learn joblib
python fusion_classifier.py
```
Produces `fusion_risk_model.pkl` (~0.98 ROC-AUC on the synthetic set).

## 3. `lstm_forecaster.py` — predictive forecasting model (TensorFlow)
Looks at the last ~10 minutes of readings and predicts whether danger is
likely in the NEXT 10 minutes — genuine forecasting, not reaction.
Needs TensorFlow, which isn't in this sandbox — run on your own machine
or Google Colab:
```
pip install tensorflow pandas numpy scikit-learn joblib
python lstm_forecaster.py
```
Produces `mine_lstm_forecaster.keras` + `lstm_scaler.pkl`.

## 4. `sensor_ai_module.py` — the edge runtime
This is what actually runs on the helmet/gateway hardware in real time.
It does fast threshold + trend-anomaly checks on every poll (works even
if the fusion/LSTM models aren't loaded), and if you pass in the trained
fusion model, it adds a fused risk probability to every report:
```python
monitor = SafetyMonitor(
    worker_ids=["W001", "W002", "W003"],
    simulate=True,  # set False + fill in real sensor drivers for hardware
    fusion_model_path="fusion_risk_model.pkl",
    fusion_features_path="fusion_feature_cols.pkl",
)
monitor.run(cycles=10)
```

## Recommended story for your SIH presentation
- **Edge layer** (`sensor_ai_module.py`): instant threshold alerts, works
  even fully offline underground with zero network.
- **Fusion layer** (`fusion_classifier.py`): combines all sensors into one
  confidence score instead of "worst sensor wins" — runs on the same
  low-power edge hardware.
- **Forecasting layer** (`lstm_forecaster.py`): the genuinely "predictive"
  piece — trained on time-series patterns to warn before a hard limit is
  crossed, run periodically at a gateway/surface station with more compute.

## Still needed before this is deployment-ready
- Swap the `SensorInterface` simulated reads for real hardware drivers once you
  know your exact sensor part numbers (I2C addresses, ADC pins, etc.)
- Replace/retrain on real mine sensor data if you can source or collect any —
  synthetic data gets you a working demo, not validated real-world accuracy
- Add worker localization (RSSI/UWB) as another fused input
- Load-test the LSTM inference time on your actual edge hardware (Pi/ESP32)
  to confirm it's fast enough to run alongside the real-time threshold checks
